fic**, aligning with the definition of a layperson synonym.   - It avoids clinical qualifiers (e.g., “narrowing,” “dilation,” “tortuosity”) that would be used in medical reports.   - The use of “abnormality” instead of “morphology” makes it more familiar and less intimidating to patients.   - It is **not overly technical**, nor is it vague—“retinal arteriolar” is a recognizable term in medical contexts, and “abnormality” is a common placeholder for any deviation.    **5. Comparative Usage:**   - While clinicians may use “abnormal retinal arterial morphology” or “retinal vascular malformation” in reports, they typically avoid “retinal arteriolar abnormality” due to its lack of specificity and potential for misinterpretation.   - Patients and families are more likely to encounter and use this phrase in the context of a diagnosis or family health history.   - In real-world communication, **patients and caregivers** are the primary users of this exact wording—especially when discussing eye findings with a doctor or reviewing a genetic report.    **Conclusion:**   The synonym *“retinal arteriolar abnormality”* is **most commonly used by patients and their families** in real-world settings. It serves as a clear, accessible, and non-technical way to describe a structural deviation in retinal blood vessels, particularly in patient education, family discussions, and clinical summaries aimed at improving health literacy. While it lacks specificity, its plain-language structure makes it effective for lay audiences and aligns with the purpose of HPO layperson synonyms. The majority of real-world use—especially in everyday communication—comes from **patients and caregivers**, not clinicians.
