130;1;assistant;2488;**Phenotype Analysis: Postaxial hand polydactyly**  **Identified Phenotype:**   Postaxial hand polydactyly is a congenital malformation characterized by the presence of an extra digit (fingers) on the lateral (postaxial) side of the hand, typically at the fifth (little) finger position. This condition involves the development of an additional digit beyond the normal number of fingers (typically five), arising from abnormal proliferation or patterning during limb development.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Specificity:** The term is highly specific to the hand, and more precisely to the postaxial (distal) side of the hand, distinguishing it from preaxial (medial) polydactyly.   - **Inclusion:** The extra digit is typically located on the ulnar side of the hand, most commonly at the level of the fifth digit. It may be fully formed, partially formed, or fused to the adjacent digit.   - **Exclusion:** This phenotype does not include polydactyly of the foot (which would be classified under "postaxial foot polydactyly"), nor does it describe preaxial polydactyly (extra digit on the radial side). It also excludes syndromic forms unless explicitly linked (e.g., in the context of syndromes like Poland syndrome or syndromic forms of limb malformations).   - **Anatomical focus:** The condition affects the digits of the hand, specifically the distal limb elements derived from the autopod.  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Developmental process:** Arises during embryogenesis due to disruptions in the signaling pathways (e.g., Sonic Hedgehog, FGF, and TGF-β pathways) that regulate digit formation and patterning in the developing limb bud.   - **Anatomical structure:** Involves the autopod (distal portion of the limb) of the upper limb.   - **Clinical relevance:** Often presents as a visible congenital anomaly, and may be isolated or part of a broader developmental syndrome. It is a well-documented phenotype in genetic disorders such as *GLI3* mutations (e.g.,