red by Patients:**   - It is grammatically simple and familiar.   - It mirrors common everyday language (e.g., “abnormality of the eyes,” “abnormality of the skin”).   - It avoids technical jargon like “morphology,” which may be unfamiliar or intimidating to non-specialists.   - It implies a deviation without specifying *how* it manifests—making it a safe, inclusive starting point for discussion.    **Comparison to Clinical Wording:**   While clinical and database terminology (e.g., “abnormal hair morphology”) is more precise and structured, “abnormality of the hair” is more accessible and emotionally resonant for patients. It allows individuals to grasp the concept without needing prior medical knowledge.    **Conclusion:**   The synonym *“abnormality of the hair”* is **most commonly used by patients and families** in real-world settings. It serves as a plain-language, inclusive, and accessible way to describe the phenotype in patient-centered communication. Though it lacks specificity, its simplicity and familiarity make it a natural choice in patient education, family discussions, and initial clinical encounters. It is not typically used by clinicians in formal documentation, where precision is essential. Thus, **patients and families account for the majority of its real-world use**.
