erred. However, in **public-facing summaries or lay summaries**, “no previous family history” is common.     - The phrase is **not typically used by clinicians in formal reports**, instead, they use more concise or technical terms like “no family history” or “sporadic.” But the full version “no previous family history” is more likely to appear in **patient-facing contexts** where clarity and emphasis on the absence of prior cases are needed.  - **Usage Frequency and Audience**:     While “no family history” is concise and common in clinical notes, **“no previous family history”** is more precise and **patient-oriented**. It explicitly references the *past* (previous) and *family*, making it more accessible and less ambiguous for non-experts.     - **Patients and caregivers** are the dominant users of this exact wording.     - It is **not commonly used by healthcare professionals in clinical documentation**—they prefer brevity and technical accuracy.     - It is **rarely found in research or phenotype databases**, where “sporadic” or “de novo” are standard.  **Conclusion**:   The synonym *"no previous family history"* is **primarily used by patients and their families** in real-world communication. It functions as a plain-language, patient-centered rewording of the HPO concept "sporadic," emphasizing the absence of familial occurrence. Its use is most prevalent in patient education, family discussions, and lay summaries, where clarity and emotional accessibility are prioritized over clinical precision. While technically accurate, it reflects the everyday language of individuals navigating a diagnosis and seeking to understand its inheritance pattern. Thus, **patients and families account for the majority of real-world use** of this exact phrase.
