owing as tall as expected.”     - “My height is below average.”     These are more natural, less technical, and better aligned with everyday speech.    **Why Not Layperson Use?**   Although *“decreased body height”* is technically a plain-language rewording, it still contains clinical phrasing (“decreased,” “body height”) that is characteristic of medical terminology. The term “decreased” is a clinical descriptor used in medical reports, not in casual conversation. It lacks the natural, experiential framing that defines true layperson synonyms (e.g., “I’m short,” “I don’t grow tall,” “I’m not as tall as others”).    **Conclusion:**   *“Decreased body height”* is **primarily used by healthcare professionals and in formal clinical and research documentation**. It is not a typical expression in patient education or everyday conversations. While it is a technically accurate and precise rewording of “short stature,” its usage is **dominated by clinicians and biomedical professionals**, not patients or families.    Thus, **healthcare professionals** account for the **majority of real-world use** of this synonym in contemporary English medical practice.
