n clinical or research contexts.    **4. Wording and Style Assessment:**   - "Breathing cessation" is grammatically natural and intuitive in everyday English.   - It avoids clinical jargon while preserving the essential meaning of a complete stop in breathing.   - It is more descriptive and concrete than "respiratory arrest," which may be unfamiliar to laypeople.   - It is not used in clinical documentation where precision and standardization are required.    **5. Conclusion:**   "Breathing cessation" is **primarily used by patients and their families** in real-world communication. It is a deliberate, effective layperson synonym designed to translate the clinical concept of respiratory arrest into accessible language. While it may appear in some clinical reports for patient clarity, its **dominant and most frequent use is in patient-facing contexts**, such as education, family discussions, and genetic counseling.    Thus, **patients and families** account for the majority of real-world use of this synonym.
