320;1;assistant;2492;**Phenotype Analysis: "Total ophthalmoplegia" (HPO Concept)**  **1. Identified Phenotype:**   Total ophthalmoplegia refers to the complete inability to move any of the extraocular muscles, resulting in the absence of voluntary eye movements in all directions. This includes both horizontal (abduction/adduction) and vertical (elevation/depression) movements, as well as rotational movements (e.g., intorsion, extorsion), depending on the specific muscle involvement.  **2. Scope and Specificity:**   - **Specificity:** The term "total" indicates that all eye movements are impaired—no residual movement is present in any direction. This distinguishes it from partial ophthalmoplegia, where some movements remain intact.   - **Inclusion:** All six extraocular muscles (superior, inferior, medial, lateral, superior oblique, and inferior oblique) are affected.   - **Exclusion:** It does not include conditions involving only one or a few muscles (e.g., isolated lateral gaze palsy), nor does it encompass non-motor eye abnormalities such as visual acuity loss or optic nerve atrophy unless directly linked.   - **Temporal aspect:** The phenotype is typically present at rest and during attempted voluntary movement, and may be static or progressive depending on the underlying cause.  **3. Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical structure:** Extraocular muscles of the orbit.   - **Physiological process:** Voluntary control of eye movement via cranial nerve innervation (primarily cranial nerves III, IV, and VI).   - **Developmental or pathological basis:** Often results from damage to the oculomotor, trochlear, or abducens nerves, or from central nervous system disorders (e.g., brainstem lesions, multiple sclerosis, mitochondrial disorders, or neurodegenerative diseases).   - **Clinical relevance:** A key feature in diagnosing disorders such as mitochondrial myopathies (e.g., MELAS, Leigh syndrome), Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease, or brainstem stroke. It may also be a fea