ontal, or positional).    **Why "Tilted lower jaw" is preferred by patients:**   - It is **visually intuitive**—people can imagine a jaw that is slanted or leaning to one side.   - It avoids technical terms like “asymmetry” or “hypoplasia,” which may be unfamiliar or confusing.   - It reflects a **common lay perception** of facial imbalance, where one side appears higher or lower than the other.   - It is **actionable and descriptive**, helping patients recognize and describe a physical feature they observe.  **Comparison to Clinical Wording:**   While “asymmetry of the mandible” is precise and used in databases and clinical assessments, “tilted lower jaw” is more accessible and emotionally resonant. It is not a direct synonym in the strictest technical sense (as “tilt” implies a specific direction and axis, which may not fully capture the full spectrum of asymmetry), but in common usage, it is understood to refer to a lateral or vertical deviation in jaw position.  **Conclusion:**   The synonym *“tilted lower jaw”* is **most commonly used by patients and families** in everyday communication, especially in patient education and informal discussions with healthcare providers. It is a layperson-friendly, intuitive rewording that effectively conveys the core visual feature of mandibular asymmetry. While not used in formal clinical or research settings, it reflects how patients naturally describe and perceive the phenotype. Therefore, **patients and their families account for the majority of real-world use** of this exact wording.
