hild of broader HPO terms such as "abnormal bone morphology" or "abnormal epiphyseal development," and may be linked to developmental disorders involving chondrocyte differentiation or bone growth regulation.  **Conclusion:**   The HPO concept "Cone-shaped epiphyses of the phalanges of the hand" describes a specific, morphological abnormality in the shape of the terminal bone ends of the hand phalanges. It reflects a developmental defect in epiphyseal formation and is a precise anatomical feature used in phenotypic characterization of skeletal disorders. The phenotype is anatomically and developmentally defined, with no implied functional or systemic consequences.
