uld be interpreted as partial absence or a clinical observation rather than developmental origin).    **Why "Absent retina" is preferred by patients/families:**   - It is intuitive and matches everyday language (e.g., “the retina is missing”).   - It avoids complex terms like “aplasia,” which may be unfamiliar or frightening.   - It aligns with how people naturally describe physical absence: “missing,” “not present,” “gone.”    **Comparison to Clinical Wording:**   - “Aplasia of the retina” is precise and technically accurate but is not used in everyday conversation.   - “Absent retina” is simpler, more direct, and conveys the same core idea without requiring medical background.    **Conclusion:**   The synonym *“absent retina”* is **most commonly used by patients and families** in real-world communication. It appears predominantly in patient education, family discussions, and accessible health resources. While some clinicians may use it in patient-facing contexts, the majority of real-world use—especially in public-facing and lay communication—is driven by patients and their caregivers. It reflects a shift toward patient-centered language in biomedical reporting and is a typical example of a successful layperson synonym in the HPO framework.
