483;1;assistant;3724;**Phenotype Analysis: Scimitar Anomaly**  **Identified Phenotype:**   Scimitar anomaly is a congenital cardiac and pulmonary malformation characterized by the presence of a partially or fully formed, right-sided, anomalous pulmonary artery that arises from the right ventricle and drains into the inferior vena cava (IVC), often with a "scimitar" (sickle-shaped) appearance due to the curved, elongated structure of the anomalous pulmonary artery and associated pulmonary venous drainage.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Anomalous origin of a right-sided pulmonary artery from the right ventricle.     - Abnormal pulmonary venous drainage (often via a single or multiple veins draining into the IVC).     - A "scimitar" shaped pulmonary artery (a key morphological feature).     - Associated with a right-sided pulmonary artery that may be partially or fully formed, with variable degrees of pulmonary vascularization.     - May include a right-sided pulmonary artery with a "sickle" or "spear" shape, typically arising from the right ventricle and coursing posteriorly to the IVC.   - **Excluded:**     - Complete absence of the right-sided pulmonary artery (this would be a different anomaly).     - Complete absence of pulmonary venous return (this would be more consistent with a different congenital heart defect).     - Isolated pulmonary hypertension or arrhythmias without structural malformation (these are secondary features, not core to the definition).    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Right ventricle, pulmonary artery, inferior vena cava, and associated pulmonary venous drainage.   - **Developmental Feature:** Abnormal development of the pulmonary arterial system during embryogenesis, specifically in the formation of the right-sided pulmonary arteries and their connection to the heart and systemic circulation.   - **Physiological Process:** Altered pulmonary blood flow due to abnormal vascular connections, potenti