e of the skeleton during development or in adulthood. It serves as a high-level descriptor that can be refined by more specific HPO terms (e.g., "scoliosis," "short stature due to skeletal dysplasia") and is used to capture structural anomalies across a wide range of genetic and developmental conditions.
