s the diagnosis.     - However, **this is not representative of accurate or standard patient-facing communication**. The correct lay synonym should reflect *protein in the urine*, not in the blood.  - **Real-World Contexts:**     - **Patient education materials:** Would typically use accurate phrasing such as “a lot of protein in your urine” or “abnormal protein in the urine.”     - **Patient-provider conversations:** Providers would say “you have a lot of protein in your urine” or “heavy proteinuria,” not “severely high blood protein levels.”     - **Genetics or phenotype databases:** Use precise terms like “proteinuria” or “heavy proteinuria” — never “blood protein levels.”     - **Biomedical publications:** Use “proteinuria” or “urinary protein excretion,” not “blood protein levels.”  **Conclusion:**   The synonym *“severely high blood protein levels”* is **not a typical or accurate way** to refer to the HPO concept “heavy proteinuria.” It misrepresents the anatomical and physiological basis of the phenotype by incorrectly placing the abnormality in the blood.    - **Primary users:** A **minority** of patients or caregivers might encounter this phrase in **inaccurate or poorly written educational materials**, but it is **not commonly or appropriately used**.   - **Majority users:** **Neither patients nor healthcare professionals** would use this exact wording in standard, accurate communication.   - **Correct lay synonym:** Should be something like *“a lot of protein in the urine”* or *“abnormally high protein in the urine.”*  ✅ **Final Assessment:**   *“Severely high blood protein levels” is not a valid or typical layperson synonym for heavy proteinuria. It is inaccurate, misleading, and does not reflect standard real-world usage in patient education, clinical practice, or biomedical communication. Its use would be rare and contextually flawed.*
