referred for consistency and interoperability.  4. **Genetics and phenotype reports:**      - In family-based or diagnostic reports, “decreased urine magnesium” may appear in the “clinical findings” section to explain a laboratory observation.      - It is used to bridge technical data and patient comprehension, especially when discussing inherited metabolic or renal disorders.  5. **Biomedical publications:**      - In scientific writing, the term is rarely used in its full form. Instead, authors use “reduced urinary magnesium excretion” or “hypomagnesiuria” for precision and consistency.      - “Decreased urine magnesium” is more common in abstracts or summaries aimed at a broader audience than in detailed research.  **Why This Wording Is Preferred by Patients:**   - It is **actionable and concrete**: “urine” is a familiar bodily fluid, and “decreased” is a common, intuitive descriptor.   - It avoids abstract or technical terms like “excretion” or “tubular reabsorption.”   - It mirrors how patients naturally describe symptoms or lab results in everyday language.  **Conclusion:**   The synonym *“decreased urine magnesium”* is **most commonly used by patients and their families** in real-world communication. While healthcare providers may use it occasionally in patient-facing notes, the majority of its use—especially in patient education, conversations, and lay summaries—comes from non-clinical audiences. It is a well-suited layperson synonym because it is accurate, accessible, and grounded in observable, tangible biological processes. Its natural flow in everyday speech makes it a strong candidate for inclusion in patient-facing HPO resources.
