560;1;assistant;2381;**Phenotype Analysis: "Subpulmonary stenosis" (HPO Concept)**  **1. Identified Phenotype:**   Subpulmonary stenosis refers to a congenital heart defect characterized by narrowing (stenosis) of the outflow tract of the right ventricle, specifically below the pulmonary valve—i.e., in the pulmonary artery or the proximal segment of the pulmonary artery. This obstruction impedes the normal flow of blood from the right ventricle to the lungs.  **2. Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Narrowing of the pulmonary artery proximal to the pulmonary valve (i.e., in the subpulmonary region).     - A structural abnormality affecting the right ventricular outflow tract.     - May involve partial or complete obstruction, with variable severity.     - Often part of a broader congenital heart disease spectrum (e.g., in tetralogy of Fallot or other right ventricular outflow tract obstructions).    - **Excluded:**     - Does not include stenosis of the pulmonary valve itself (which is classified as "pulmonary valve stenosis").     - Does not refer to stenosis in the left ventricular outflow tract (e.g., aortic stenosis).     - Does not describe functional or acquired stenosis (e.g., from inflammation or atherosclerosis), it is primarily a congenital structural anomaly.    **3. Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Right ventricular outflow tract (RVOT), specifically the proximal pulmonary artery.   - **Physiological Process:** Impaired forward flow of deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the pulmonary circulation.   - **Developmental Feature:** A malformation of the cardiac outflow tract during embryogenesis, typically arising from incomplete septation or abnormal development of the pulmonary artery.   - **Clinical Relevance:** Often detected prenatally or at birth via echocardiography, may present with symptoms such as cyanosis, hypoxia, or poor growth. Can be isolated or part of a syndromic condition (e.g., in syndromes involving cardiac malformations).    **Conclusion:**   "Subpulmonary stenosis" describes a congenital narrowing of the pulmonary artery proximal to the pulmonary valve, impairing right ventricular ejection into the pulmonary circulation. It is a specific structural cardiac anomaly with defined anatomical and developmental context, distinct from pulmonary valve stenosis or other outflow tract obstructions.
