lified summaries of a diagnosis or syndrome.   - **Patient-provider conversations:** Commonly used by patients to describe their symptoms or observations.   - **Genetics reports:** Rarely used directly, instead, more precise terms like “enlarged maxilla” or “maxillary hyperplasia” are preferred.   - **Biomedical publications:** Not used at all—too imprecise and non-standard.   - **Phenotype databases:** Not used as a formal label, the HPO term remains technical.    **Conclusion:**   “Upper jaw excess” is a **patient-facing, plain-language synonym** designed to be easily understood by individuals without medical training. It is **most commonly used by patients and their families** in informal, real-world communication. While it captures the essence of the phenotype, it lacks the precision of clinical terminology and is not appropriate for formal medical documentation. Its primary function is to bridge the gap between clinical data and patient understanding.    👉 **Majority user group: Patients and families**.
