inical symptom, abnormalities in the serosa may be observed in conditions such as intestinal malrotation, congenital diaphragmatic hernia, or certain syndromes involving mesodermal or peritoneal defects (e.g., in some forms of intestinal atresia or peritoneal adhesions).    **Conclusion:**   The HPO concept "serosa of intestine" describes a structural anatomical feature of the intestinal wall. Its intended meaning is the normal or abnormal presence, thickness, or integrity of the serosal layer. It is a precise, anatomically grounded phenotype relevant to developmental biology and gastrointestinal anatomy, and should be interpreted in the context of structural defects in the intestinal wall rather than functional or symptomatic manifestations.
