atterns and Wording Preferences:**   - The phrase "pupil of right eye" is more descriptive and spatially explicit than "right pupil," which can be ambiguous without context.   - It is not used in clinical documentation for routine notes (where brevity and standardization are valued), but rather in patient-centered contexts where clarity and accessibility are paramount.   - The addition of "of the right eye" provides a natural, grammatical structure that aligns with how people describe body parts in daily speech.  **Comparison to Clinical Wording:**   - Clinical terms like "abnormality of the right pupil" or "right pupil dilation" are used in medical records and databases.   - "Pupil of right eye" avoids the passive or abstract tone of such terms and instead uses active, locative language that mirrors how patients naturally refer to body parts.  **Conclusion:**   The synonym **"pupil of right eye"** is most commonly used by **patients and their families** in real-world communication. It is a natural, accessible, and spatially clear way to refer to the anatomical structure in everyday language. Its use is prevalent in patient education, family discussions, and genetic reports written for non-specialist audiences. While clinicians may use it occasionally for clarity, the majority of real-world usage—especially in lay contexts—comes from patients and caregivers. Therefore, **patients and families** account for the majority of its use.
