tomy (e.g., as per the Anatomical Terminology, ICD-10-AM, or anatomical databases). It is not a clinical symptom, process, or functional deficit. Its use in HPO is likely in the context of describing structural abnormalities (e.g., hypoplasia, malformation, or absence) of the iliac blade in disease contexts.  **Conclusion**:   The HPO concept "iliac blade" describes a normal anatomical structure of the pelvis. It is not a disease phenotype but a structural component. Its intended meaning is the anatomical region of the ilium that forms the posterior, flat portion of the hip bone, involved in structural and biomechanical support. It is specific and limited to anatomical description, and should be interpreted in the context of structural variation or malformation when linked to disease.
