se plain, accurate language such as “outer layer of the eye,” “surface of the cornea,” or “protective layer of the eye.”      - **Patient-provider conversations:** Never. Patients would not hear or use “epithelium posterius corneae” — it is too obscure, technically incorrect, and not meaningful to laypeople.      - **Clinical documentation (e.g., EHRs, notes):** Rarely. Even in detailed medical records, clinicians use “corneal epithelium,” “corneal surface,” or “epithelial defect,” not the Latinized, incorrect term.      - **Genetics reports or phenotype databases:** Not typically. HPO uses standardized, accurate terms. While some databases may include synonyms for completeness, “epithelium posterius corneae” would be flagged as erroneous or deprecated.      - **Biomedical publications:** Very rare. In scientific literature, the correct term is “corneal epithelium.” The use of “posterior” in this context would be a mistake or a misstatement.  4. **Layperson Synonym Status:**      - This term **does not meet the criteria** for a layperson synonym as defined by HPO.      - Layperson synonyms are intended to be **clear, accurate, and accessible** to patients and families.      - “Epithelium posterius corneae” is **not plain language**, is **anatomically incorrect**, and would **confuse** rather than clarify.      - It is not used in any real-world communication setting involving patients or even routine clinical practice.  **Conclusion:**   The synonym *“epithelium posterius corneae”* is **not typically used** in real-world communication to refer to the HPO concept *“corneal epithelium.”* It is an anatomically inaccurate and misleading term that misidentifies the layer of the cornea.    - **Primary users (if any):** A very small number of specialized researchers or anatomists in historical or technical contexts, but not in clinical or patient-facing settings.   - **Majority of real-world use:** **None** — the term is not used by patients, clinicians, or in standard documentation.   - **Status:** This synonym is **not appropriate** for inclusion as a layperson synonym in HPO. It should be reviewed or corrected to reflect accurate anatomical terminology (e.g., “corneal epithelium” or “posterior epithelial layer” only if referring to a specific, well-defined structure).    **Final Assessment:**   ❌ *“Epithelium posterius corneae” is not a typical or valid synonym for “corneal epithelium” in real-world usage.*   It is anatomically incorrect, contextually misleading, and not used by patients or clinicians in standard communication. It fails to meet the criteria for a layperson synonym and should be revised or removed.
